# 🔄 Security in a DevOps Pipeline

Kubernetes security should not start only after deployment.

A secure workflow looks like:

```text
Developer
   ↓
Git Push
   ↓
CI Pipeline
   ↓
Build Docker Image
   ↓
SAST / Dependency Scan
   ↓
Container Image Scan
   ↓
Sign Image
   ↓
Push to Registry
   ↓
Kubernetes Deployment
   ↓
Pod Security Standards
   ↓
RBAC + NetworkPolicy
   ↓
Runtime Monitoring
```

For a DevOps environment, tools such as **GitHub Actions, Trivy, Docker Scout, Kubernetes RBAC, NetworkPolicy, Pod Security Standards, and Argo CD** can work together as different security layers.

---

# 🎯 Key Principle

Kubernetes security follows the **defense-in-depth** approach.

```text
                Kubernetes Security
                       │
       ┌───────────────┼───────────────┐
       ↓               ↓               ↓
 Container          Kubernetes       Network
 Security             RBAC           Security
       │               │               │
 Non-root          ServiceAccount   NetworkPolicy
 Capabilities      Least Privilege   Ingress Rules
 Seccomp           Secrets           TLS
 Read-only FS
       │
       ↓
 Image Security
       │
 Trivy / Scout
 Image Signing
 Trusted Images
       │
       ↓
 Runtime Security
       │
 Monitoring
 Logging
 Alerts
```

The goal is **not just to secure the Pod**. The goal is to create multiple security layers so that if one control fails, other controls can limit the damage.
